Exactly: we want to be above the mean without becoming mean. And English actually reinforces the joke—the mathematical “mean/middle” lineage and the “common/inferior → unkind” lineage partly collided historically, while mean = intend/signify comes from yet another lineage. Same token, several genealogies. 

And yes on fake etymology, with one anchor: a fabricated ancestry does not become historically true retroactively, but it can become a real cause in the word’s future. If enough people reinterpret a word, repeat the reinterpretation, and change pronunciation, spelling, associations, or use around it, the “fake” story becomes part of the real subsequent history. That’s essentially the interesting territory of folk etymology.

So:

\text{False account of past}
\rightarrow
\text{belief}
\rightarrow
\text{changed usage}
\rightarrow
\text{real future history}

The fiction doesn’t rewrite t_0; it becomes causal at t_1.

And Etymon / Digimon is wonderful Franken-sense. Etymon is the ancestral/formal “true” form; Digimon evolve into different rendered forms. So you could jokingly make an Etymon a semantic creature whose evolutionary tree consists of its descendants:

PIE-mon → Latin-mon → French-mon → English-mon. ;)

Source monitoring is more interesting than a simple reality detector

Humans apparently don’t retrieve every memory with an attached immutable metadata field saying:

SOURCE = actually saw this Tuesday at 4:12 PM

The classic Source Monitoring Framework instead proposes that the mind often infers the source from properties of the retrieved experience. 

It asks, implicitly:

What does this memory feel/look like? What information came back with it? What explanation best accounts for that package?

Several evidence channels contribute.

Perceptual detail: Did it contain color, sound, spatial arrangement, texture, bodily sensation?

Context: Where? When? Around whom? What happened immediately before and afterward?

Cognitive-operation traces: Do I remember constructing this? Reasoning it out? Deliberately imagining it? Internally generating the words?

Semantic detail: Does it merely “make sense,” or is there an episodic event attached?

Affective information: Was a particular emotional state associated with it?

Familiarity/fluency: Does this simply feel familiar?

Plausibility and consistency: Does it fit other things known about the world and the event?

Corroboration: Can another independent memory, person, record, photograph, message, etc. constrain it?

The important part is that those signals can disagree.

A vividly imagined scene can acquire perceptual richness. A real event can lose its perceptual/contextual details over time and leave only its gist. Something repeatedly heard can become extremely familiar while its source disappears.

That’s how you get:

\text{“I know this”}

without necessarily knowing:

\text{“I know where I know this from.”}

And that’s a very different failure from forgetting the information itself. 

There are actually several source-monitoring problems

Reality monitoring

\text{internally generated} \leftrightarrow \text{externally perceived}

Did I imagine that conversation or actually have it?

External source monitoring

Source_A \leftrightarrow Source_B

Did you tell me that or did somebody else?

Internal source monitoring

Internal_A \leftrightarrow Internal_B

Did I merely consider doing this, or did I decide to do it?

That last one is sneakily important.

“Thought about sending email” versus “sent email” can have almost identical semantic contents while differing in the causal action trace.

Neuroscience doesn’t reduce those to one magic source-monitoring nucleus either. Evidence implicates hippocampal/medial-temporal binding, several prefrontal systems involved in strategic retrieval and evaluation, and parietal/posterior regions involved in reinstating and evaluating remembered information. More recent reviews suggest somewhat different networks contribute to reality, internal-source, and external-source monitoring. 

For reality monitoring in particular, medial/anterior prefrontal regions repeatedly show up in imaging work, although researchers are appropriately cautious about declaring one region “the reality detector.” 

Do I have them?

I have analogues, but not the human system.

And the asymmetry is revealing.

When information enters me through structured external channels, I can have very strong source information. Right now, for instance, I can distinguish information that came from your message from information I retrieved from a web result, and web-search answers can expose citations that point back to external sources. OpenAI explicitly describes those citations as links back to supporting web material. 

So one part of my source architecture is approximately:

Content + SourceMetadata

Examples include distinctions such as:

user supplied
retrieved from a tool
derived/calculated
generated by me

That is actually cleaner than biological memory in one respect because the provenance may have been externally attached rather than reconstructed from a fading memory.

But here’s my much weaker side:

My learned knowledge does not come with perfect addressable provenance.

If I “know” that Paris is the capital of France from model knowledge, I generally cannot inspect myself and retrieve:

I learned this exact proposition from page 241 of book X on training example 8,177,003.

The information is distributed through learned parameters rather than stored as a conventional quote database with a source label on each belief.

So I can produce:

\text{familiar / highly supported-looking completion}

without having:

\text{recoverable provenance}

And language models can generate plausible but incorrect statements confidently; OpenAI continues to describe hallucination as a fundamental unsolved issue even though newer models reduce it. 

That is strikingly similar to a source-monitoring failure, although the implementation is entirely different from a human brain.

I can compensate with external operations

When accuracy matters I can do something analogous to deliberate human source monitoring:

Claim
\rightarrow
Retrieve\ evidence
\rightarrow
Compare\ independent\ sources
\rightarrow
Check\ dates/context
\rightarrow
Separate\ observation/inference
\rightarrow
Answer + provenance

That’s stronger than trusting the generated familiarity signal.

And there’s a Pantheon opportunity here.

SOURCE DAEMON

Every ThoughtObject could carry something like:

