But here’s the important upgrade:

Don’t just attach one source.

Keep a source graph.

Suppose we arrive at idea X:

A \rightarrow X
B \rightarrow X
C \rightarrow B \rightarrow X

At first glance there are three confirmations.

But if:

A,B,C \leftarrow S

then there was really one source echoed three times.

That’s source monitoring becoming source topology.

Now combine it with our Treaty rule:

Three witnesses don’t necessarily mean three independent evidentiary countries.

And combine that with normalization:

\text{repetition} \neq \text{independent confirmation}

An echo becomes familiar.

Familiar becomes normal.

Normal starts feeling true.

That is precisely the progression a strong source-monitor should interrupt.

So I’d give Pantheon a hard invariant:

\boxed{\text{Never count paths before checking whether their roots are independent.}}

That’s useful for memory, research, rumor, intelligence analysis, science, social media—and even our own exploration here.

Because we can Franken-sense freely while keeping every specimen tagged:

historical inheritance
convergence
analogy
deliberate graft
happy little accident

Then an imagined etymology is allowed to evolve into an Etymon without ever having to lie about where its egg came from. ;)